{"title":"SSS similarity","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Theorem\">Theorem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=SSS_similarity&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Theorem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/b/5/fb5da4fc8c79af2eea417be20e6254514dbb760d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{SSS Similarity states that if there are two triangles (shown below), then if }\\dfrac{\\overline{AB}}{\\overline{DE}}=\\dfrac{\\overline{BC}}{\\overline{EF}}=\\dfrac{\\overline{AC}}{\\overline{DF}}\\text{, then the two triangles are similar.}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -15px\" width=\"953\" height=\"44\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/2/7/827884924fc2c7e934b733847458b27667f41b6c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] pair A,B,C,D,E,F;  A = (4,0);  B = (2,0);  C = (3,1);  D = (6,0);  E = (5,0);  F = (5.5,0.5);  draw(A--B);  draw(B--C);  draw(A--C);  draw(D--E);  draw(E--F);  draw(D--F);  dot(&quot;$A$&quot;,A,NE);  dot(&quot;$B$&quot;,B,NW);  dot(&quot;$C$&quot;,C,N);  dot(&quot;$D$&quot;,D,NE);  dot(&quot;$E$&quot;,E,NW);  dot(&quot;$F$&quot;,F,N); [/asy]\" width=\"252\" height=\"78\" />\n</p><p><i>This article is a stub.  Help us out by <span class=\"plainlinks\"><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=SSS_similarity&amp;action=edit\">expanding it</a></span>.</i>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=SSS_similarity&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<ul><li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=AAA_Similarity&a